<p><strong>Thank you for your critique.</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Explaining&nbsp;my&nbsp;Plot&nbsp;Holes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I understand where you’re coming from with my cross-references being mostly from Series I, but I haven’t found any other SCPs to fill the role of Zeus (SCP-343), Hermes (SCP-001 leading Orpheus), or an entrance into the underworld (the Gate at Site 0). I feel like JALAKARA is a good pick for Hades and Eurydice being an O5 member would be a good reason as to why the Foundation is even involved.</p>
<p>In terms of the Abrahamic Figures, I do have a distaste for using these SCPs instead of Greek anomalies, but I think introducing the Greek Figures without a defined SCP would make the file too cluttered with unlisted anomalies. I personally don’t find it satisfying to reference an entity without having an already made file of the anomaly. But something that can be explained as in the custody of another GoI (like Cerberus) is accepted. But these Greek Gods would most likely be in the custody of the Foundation or at least beyond the Foundation’s control (JALAKARA).</p>
<p>In terms of the Serpent’s Hand, they do serve two purposes to the story (something I couldn’t explain due to the word limit). 1) They obviously want to take down the Foundation, and a distracted O5 member would make easy prey; 2) they are now tying up the loose end of Orpheus, as he is the only person able to resurrect Eurydice. Orpheus had the goal to resurrect her, and once he failed all he knew was that she was dead. To be with her, it meant he had to die.</p>
<p>I feel like the thread is missing the aspect of Orpheus’ motivation: <strong>to simply resurrect Eurydice.</strong><br />
He has no reason to destroy the Serpent’s Hand or ask the Foundation for help, he simply wants Eurydice back. Once he fails his trial and looks back, he has the need to kill himself to see her in the afterlife. That was the goal for Orpheus and his death, but of course I couldn’t explain this due to the word limit.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
I hope this explains the plot holes you have noted and I hope you find this post informative on the story. If you want me to still flush out certain aspects, I am willing to do so, but I feel like this post is the one that explains the plot holes.
<p><em>Again, thank you for the critique.</em></p>

