<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Placeholder! Got asked to take another look. Will crit in a bit.</span></p>
<p>Alright, here’s my take.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Let’s start with the anomaly. At the moment, I’m seeing four elements:</p>
<ul>
<li>Manifestations of the dead</li>
<li>Cognitohazards on the walls</li>
<li>Rooms with ‘outcomes’ of their choices</li>
<li>A wish-granting figure</li>
</ul>
<p>Knowing the origin of this concept, I can see how these are being used to approximate the Hades from the story; in-universe, however, this is a mix of random anomalous attributes that don’t relate to each other. What does the wish-granter have to do with the cognitohazards? Why does it grant wishes? Frankly, it might be easier to just modify the concept of the Greek underworld instead of trying to create one from scratch like this.</p>
<p>As for the story, it seems like there’s a lot of plot points that are unnecessary. The core concept is certainly there (higher-up tries to invade Hell to get their loved one back, mass casualties, loved one lost on the way back) but the combination of cognitohazards AND demons makes for an odd mix of casualties that might confuse the reader.</p>
<p>I’m also not sure why it has to be a member of the O5 council. Considering their relative secrecy and power, wouldn’t a member of the Council just send in the team on their own?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
If you respond to this comment and would like me to see it, please send me a Wikidot PM, as I will not be notified otherwise.
